<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="v2rayN.app" />

  <!--
    No DPI block here, on purpose.

    Declaring PerMonitorV2 without the matching WinForms scaling switch makes the
    process DPI-aware, so Windows stops bitmap-stretching the window, but .NET
    Framework WinForms does not rescale anything itself. Every control then
    renders at 1/1.5 or 1/2 physical size: sharp, and far too small. That is
    worse than the DPI-unaware default this build shipped with for years, which
    is blurry but correctly sized.

    So it is left off entirely rather than half configured. Doing it properly
    needs app.config's System.Windows.Forms.ApplicationConfigurationSection with
    both DpiAwareness and EnableWindowsFormsHighDpiAutoResizing, and it has to be
    checked on a real 150% and 200% display before shipping: a CI runner at
    100% cannot see any of this.

    The supportedOS block below is the part that matters, and it has no effect on
    layout: without it Environment.OSVersion reports 6.2 on every machine, which
    is what made GetCheckUpdateCoreTypes hide Xray, mihomo and sing-box.
  -->

  <!--
    supportedOS. Without a declaration, .NET Framework's Environment.OSVersion
    returns 6.2 (Windows 8) on every machine, whatever is really running.

    That is not cosmetic here: CoreInfoManager.GetCheckUpdateCoreTypes() reads

        if (!(Utils.IsWindows() && Environment.OSVersion.Version.Major < 10))

    so with a hardcoded 6.2 the test is false everywhere and Xray, mihomo and
    sing-box are dropped from the update list on every version of Windows.

    Declaring Windows 10 also stops Windows 10/11 lying to us. Per Microsoft it
    has no effect when the app runs on an earlier OS, so listing Windows 7
    alongside it does not cost Windows 7 its own compatibility behaviour.
  -->
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{1f676c76-80e1-4239-95bb-83d0f6d0da78}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{4a2f28e3-53b9-4441-ba9c-d69d4a4a6e38}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{35138b9a-5d96-4fbd-8e2d-a2440225f93a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
